%[text] # What's New in R2026b?
%[text] [![](text:image:6447)](https://matlab.mathworks.com/open/github/v1?repo=simulink/blog&project=2026_09_23_R2026b/matlab.toml)
%[text] MATLAB R2026b is now available. Here are my favorite enhancements.
%%
%[text] ## Published Referenced Models
%[text] In R2026b, you will notice a new **Publish** button in the Simulink toolstrip:
%[text] ![](text:image:52d7)
%[text] When publishing a model, a new SLXP file is created. This file contains a precompiled, ready-to-run version of the model. As a result, models that reference a published model can initialize quickly by skipping [rebuild checks](https://blogs.mathworks.com/simulink/2023/10/18/accelerator-cache-and-rebuilds-everything-you-need-to-know) and using the precompiled version in the SLXP file.
%[text] Publishing can also be done programmatically using [`publishmodel`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/rtw/ref/publishmodel.html).
%[text] A Model block that references a published model displays a new badge:
%[text] ![](text:image:4815)
%%
%[text] ## MATLAB Project definition file
%[text] This enhancement is not specific to Simulink, but I expect many Simulink users to like it. 
%[text] Before R2026b, MATLAB projects stored project definition information in many XML files in a resources folder. This made the metadata difficult to inspect and added noise to source-control diffs. In R2026b, you can instead store this information in a single, human-readable `matlab.toml` file at the top level of the project.
%[text] ![](text:image:5e2f)
%[text] This is controlled in the MATLAB settings:
%[text] ![](text:image:549d)
%[text] If you want to convert an existing project, you can execute:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] matlab.project.convertDefinitionFiles(pwd,matlab.project.DefinitionFiles.Toml)
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] ## Save Simulink Data Dictionaries in JSON format
%[text] Before R2026b, Simulink Data Dictionary files were saved in a binary format. In R2026b, we are adding an option to save data dictionaries as an uncompressed text file in a JSON format. Many users may not notice a difference in their day-to-day workflows, but for teams using Git, this option should help keep repository sizes manageable and simplify diff workflows.
%[text] The data dictionary format is also controlled from the MATLAB settings:
%[text] ![](text:image:1c7a)
%%
%[text] ## Simulink comparison results available at the MATLAB Command Prompt
%[text] When calling [`visdiff`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/visdiff.html) on two Simulink models, the output now contains a table with all the changes.
result = visdiff('vdp1.slx','vdp2.slx');
result.Result.Matches %[output:5e751018]
%[text] This is especially useful when using a coding agent connected to MATLAB through the MCP server. Here is an example where I asked [Codex CLI](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/codex/cli) to summarize the above changes:
%[text] ![](text:image:3008)
%[text] ## Tabs in Simulation Data Inspector
%[text] The Simulation Data Inspector now has tabs, making it easier to organize and view large number of signals.
%[text] ![](text:image:2983)
%[text] ## Custom Simulink Solvers
%[text] It is now possible to write your own solver for Simulink models. This can potentially improve speed and/or accuracy when dealing with specialized sets of equations that could be solved more efficiently using a special solver algorithms not included with Simulink.
%[text] See this example: [Implement Stabilized Variable-Step Plugin Solver Using Chebyshev Polynomials - MATLAB & Simulink](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/slref/implement-stabilized-variable-step-plugin-solver-using-chebyshev-polynomials.html)
%[text] This feature is also useful if you want to impress your friends and colleagues by having your name appear in the list of solvers available in Simulink!
%[text] ![](text:image:09af)
%[text] ## Now it's your turn
%[text] Go through the [Simulink release notes](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/release-notes.html) and let us know your favorite enhancements and which topics you would like to see covered in more detail on this blog.

